---
import PlaygroundShell from '../components/PlaygroundShell.astro';
import RangeField from '../components/RangeField.astro';
---
<PlaygroundShell title="Simulate it" hint="Run six connection attempts through a rule set, then load a NAT gateway with a small port pool.">
  <ol class="how">
    <li>Choose <b>Stateless ACL, inbound rules only</b>: 3 requests reach the server but <b>0</b> connections work, because the replies have no outbound rule. Choose <b>Stateless ACL, with return rule</b> and 3 work.</li>
    <li>Compare <b>ACL, deny /24 listed first</b> (2 work) with <b>ACL, deny /24 listed second</b> (3 work): same rules, different order.</li>
    <li>Choose <b>Stateful group, allow all to test</b>: 5 connections work and the admin ports <b>22,5432</b> become reachable from the internet.</li>
    <li>In the NAT panel leave <b>New connections per tick</b> at 4 and <b>Hold time (ticks)</b> at 3: the peak is 12 ports and 0 are dropped. Set <b>Hold time (ticks)</b> to 6: it needs 24, the pool of 20 fills and <b>20</b> connections are dropped. Set <b>NAT pool (ports)</b> to <b>40</b> and nothing is dropped.</li>
  </ol>
  <div class="pg natfw" data-natfw>
    <div class="pg-row">
      <label>Rule set
        <select class="pg-in" data-cfg>
          <option value="0" selected>Stateless ACL, inbound rules only</option>
          <option value="1">Stateless ACL, with return rule</option>
          <option value="2">Stateful group, same allow rules</option>
          <option value="3">ACL, deny /24 listed first</option>
          <option value="4">ACL, deny /24 listed second</option>
          <option value="5">Stateful group, allow all to test</option>
        </select>
      </label>
    </div>
    <p class="pg-note" data-rules></p>
    <div class="natfw-wrap" tabindex="0" role="region" aria-label="Result for each connection attempt">
      <table class="natfw-table">
        <thead><tr><th scope="col">From</th><th scope="col">Port</th><th scope="col">Request</th><th scope="col">Reply</th><th scope="col">Result</th></tr></thead>
        <tbody data-rows></tbody>
      </table>
    </div>
    <div class="pg-grid">
      <div class="pg-box" style="--c: var(--role-1)"><h3>Requests reaching the server</h3><span class="pg-mono" data-reached>0</span></div>
      <div class="pg-box" style="--c: var(--role-2)"><h3>Connections that work</h3><span class="pg-mono" data-works>0</span></div>
      <div class="pg-box" style="--c: var(--role-3)"><h3>Admin ports open to the internet</h3><span class="pg-mono" data-exposed>none</span></div>
    </div>
    <p class="pg-out" data-verdict aria-live="polite"></p>
    <h3>NAT port pool</h3>
    <div class="pg-row">
      <label>NAT pool (ports) <select class="pg-in" data-pool><option value="20" selected>20</option><option value="40">40</option></select></label>
      <RangeField label="New connections per tick" name="rate" min="1" max="10" step="1" value="4" readout="4" />
      <RangeField label="Hold time (ticks)" name="hold" min="1" max="10" step="1" value="3" readout="3" />
    </div>
    <div class="pg-grid">
      <div class="pg-box" style="--c: var(--role-4)"><h3>Ports needed</h3><span class="pg-mono" data-need>0</span><span class="pg-note">rate x hold</span></div>
      <div class="pg-box" style="--c: var(--role-5)"><h3>Peak in use</h3><span class="pg-mono" data-peak>0</span></div>
      <div class="pg-box" style="--c: var(--bad)"><h3>Connections dropped</h3><span class="pg-mono" data-dropped>0</span><span class="pg-note" data-dnote></span></div>
    </div>
    <p class="pg-note">Model: 30 ticks. Each tick, ports whose hold expired are freed, then up to the new-connection rate are allocated from what is free; the rest are dropped. A real public address has at most about 64,000 ports per destination (an AWS NAT gateway allows up to 55,000 simultaneous connections per address to each unique destination), and timeouts vary by vendor. The filters are the six sample rule sets: inbound rules match the sender and the local port, replies are checked against outbound rules (stateless) or ride the connection table (stateful).</p>
  </div>
</PlaygroundShell>
<script>
  import { query, numberValue } from '../playground';
  type Rule = [string, string, number, number];
  const ANY = '0.0.0.0/0';
  const RETURN: Rule = ['allow', ANY, 1024, 65535];
  const IN_BASE: Rule[] = [['allow', ANY, 443, 443], ['allow', '10.0.0.0/16', 5432, 5432]];
  const CONFIGS: { name: string; stateful: boolean; inRules: Rule[]; outRules: Rule[]; text: string }[] = [
    { name: 'stateless ACL, inbound rules only', stateful: false, inRules: IN_BASE, outRules: [], text: 'Inbound: allow 443 from anywhere, allow 5432 from 10.0.0.0/16. Outbound: nothing.' },
    { name: 'stateless ACL, with return rule', stateful: false, inRules: IN_BASE, outRules: [RETURN], text: 'Same inbound rules. Outbound: allow ports 1024 to 65535.' },
    { name: 'stateful group, same allow rules', stateful: true, inRules: IN_BASE, outRules: [], text: 'Same inbound allow rules, no outbound rule: replies ride the connection table.' },
    { name: 'ACL, deny /24 listed first', stateful: false, inRules: [['deny', '203.0.113.0/24', 0, 65535], ...IN_BASE], outRules: [RETURN], text: 'Rule 1 denies 203.0.113.0/24, then the two allows. Return rule present.' },
    { name: 'ACL, deny /24 listed second', stateful: false, inRules: [IN_BASE[0], ['deny', '203.0.113.0/24', 0, 65535], IN_BASE[1]], outRules: [RETURN], text: 'Allow 443 first, then deny 203.0.113.0/24, then allow 5432 from inside. Return rule present.' },
    { name: 'stateful group, allow all to test', stateful: true, inRules: [['allow', ANY, 0, 65535]], outRules: [], text: 'One rule: allow every port from 0.0.0.0/0.' },
  ];
  const LISTENING = [443, 22, 5432];
  const EPHEMERAL = 50000;
  const ATTEMPTS: [string, number][] = [['203.0.113.5', 443], ['198.51.100.7', 443], ['203.0.113.5', 22], ['10.0.1.9', 5432], ['203.0.113.9', 5432], ['192.0.2.44', 80]];
  const OCTET = 256, IPV4_BITS = 32, NAT_TICKS = 30;
  const ipv = (s: string) => s.split('.').reduce((acc, o) => acc * OCTET + Number(o), 0);
  function inCidr(addr: number, cidr: string) { const [base, bits] = cidr.split('/'); const size = 2 ** (IPV4_BITS - Number(bits)); const b = ipv(base); return addr >= b && addr < b + size; }
  function evaluate(rules: Rule[], peer: string, port: number) {
    for (const [action, cidr, lo, hi] of rules) if (inCidr(ipv(peer), cidr) && port >= lo && port <= hi) return action === 'allow';
    return false;
  }
  function natRun(pool: number, rate: number, hold: number, ticks: number) {
    const expiring = new Array(ticks + hold + 1).fill(0);
    let used = 0, allocated = 0, dropped = 0, peak = 0;
    for (let t = 0; t < ticks; t++) {
      used -= expiring[t];
      const got = Math.min(rate, pool - used);
      used += got; allocated += got; dropped += rate - got; expiring[t + hold] += got; peak = Math.max(peak, used);
    }
    return { allocated, dropped, peak };
  }

  document.querySelectorAll<HTMLElement>('[data-natfw]').forEach((root) => {
    const q = query(root); const val = numberValue(q);
    const setText = (s: string, v: string) => { q(s).textContent = v; };
    function draw() {
      const c = CONFIGS[val('[data-cfg]')];
      setText('[data-rules]', c.text);
      const rows = q('[data-rows]'); rows.textContent = '';
      let works = 0, reached = 0; const exposed: number[] = [];
      for (const [client, port] of ATTEMPTS) {
        const request = evaluate(c.inRules, client, port);
        const reply = c.stateful ? request : evaluate(c.outRules, client, EPHEMERAL);
        if (request) reached++;
        const ok = request && reply && LISTENING.includes(port);
        if (ok) { works++; if (!client.startsWith('10.') && port !== 443) exposed.push(port); }
        const result = ok ? 'works' : !request ? 'blocked inbound' : !reply ? 'reply dropped' : 'port closed';
        const tr = document.createElement('tr');
        [client, String(port), request ? 'allowed' : 'denied', request ? (reply ? 'allowed' : 'dropped') : '-', result].forEach((t) => { const td = document.createElement('td'); td.textContent = t; tr.appendChild(td); });
        rows.appendChild(tr);
      }
      setText('[data-reached]', String(reached)); setText('[data-works]', String(works));
      setText('[data-exposed]', exposed.length ? exposed.join(',') : 'none');
      setText('[data-verdict]', exposed.length ? 'SSH and the database answer the whole internet. Nothing is failing, so nothing prompts anyone to fix it.'
        : reached > works ? `${reached} requests arrive but only ${works} connections complete: the missing piece is the reply path.` : `${works} connections work and no admin port is exposed.`);
      const rate = val('[data-rate]'), hold = val('[data-hold]'), pool = val('[data-pool]');
      setText('[data-rateval]', String(rate)); setText('[data-holdval]', String(hold));
      const r = natRun(pool, rate, hold, NAT_TICKS);
      setText('[data-need]', String(rate * hold)); setText('[data-peak]', String(r.peak)); setText('[data-dropped]', String(r.dropped));
      setText('[data-dnote]', r.dropped ? `of ${rate * NAT_TICKS} attempted in ${NAT_TICKS} ticks` : 'pool is large enough');
    }
    root.querySelectorAll('select, input').forEach((el) => el.addEventListener('input', draw));
    draw();
  });
</script>
<style is:global>
  .playground .natfw .natfw-wrap { overflow-x: auto; margin: 12px 0; }
  .playground .natfw .natfw-table { border-collapse: collapse; width: 100%; font-size: .9rem; }
  .playground .natfw .natfw-table th, .playground .natfw .natfw-table td { border-bottom: 1px solid var(--border); padding: 6px 10px; text-align: left; white-space: nowrap; }
</style>
